    "faq_branzino": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_skin": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "My branzino skin curled up and never crisped.",
            "text": "<p>It lifted off the pan in the first few seconds, which is what thin skin does. Press the fillets flat with a fish spatula for the first thirty seconds, until they stop fighting you, then leave them alone for three to four minutes. The other two causes are a damp fillet and a pan that was not hot enough when the fish went in.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_butter": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I just sear it in butter?",
            "text": "<p>Not at this heat. Butter's milk solids burn well before the skin has set, so you get a bitter pan and pale skin. Sear in olive oil, then add the butter after you flip and baste for the last minute &mdash; you get the flavour without the scorching.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_farro": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How long does the farro actually take?",
            "text": "<p>It depends entirely on which you bought, and the range is wide enough to wreck your timing: roughly 15 minutes for pearled, 25 for semi-pearled and 40 for whole. Check the packet before you start the rest, and cook it until tender but still chewy.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_ahead": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I make this for the week?",
            "text": "<p>Partly. The farro salad keeps four days refrigerated if you hold back the pear, parsley and hazelnuts, and it genuinely improves after a night. Cooked fish does not keep that long &mdash; three days is the limit, and if you fold the fish through the salad then three days is the limit for the whole container.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_maitake": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What if I can't find maitake?",
            "text": "<p>Oyster mushrooms are the closest, torn the same way so they keep those frilly edges that catch the oil and brown. Cremini work too, quartered, though there is less surface area so you get less of the crisp bits. Whatever you use, give it four to five minutes undisturbed before you touch it.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_thaw": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How should I thaw Seatopia seafood?",
            "text": "<p>Overnight in the refrigerator in the sealed pack, about 12 hours, then pat it very dry. In a hurry, submerge the sealed pack in a bowl of chilled water for 20 to 30 minutes, changing the water once. Never at room temperature &mdash; and for this recipe especially, never skip the patting dry.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_skin",
        "q_butter",
        "q_farro",
        "q_ahead",
        "q_maitake",
        "q_thaw"
      ],
